Omarova and Graham Steele', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Saule', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Omarova', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Graham', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Steele', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c3f9e4e-0085-5ac1-9ed9-8ade4df25985', 'word_count': 1571, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c3f9e4e-0085-5ac1-9ed9-8ade4df25985'}, {'abstract': 'Chess helps answer the perennial human question, “What should I do next?”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/11/04/opinion/happiness-chess-joy.html', 'snippet': 'Chess helps answer the perennial human question, “What should I do next?”', 'lead_paragraph': 'You may have already figured out that our inalienable right to pursue happiness is self-defeating. Happiness may be with us, baked into our present moment, eagerly awaiting our grateful acknowledgment, but nothing is less likely to make us happy than trying to pursue it. On this analysis, we are right to desire happiness; it’s just that the predatory process of chasing it drives what we apparently want out of reach.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/06/opinion/04stone-rowson/04stone-rowson-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/11/06/opinion/04stone-rowson/04stone-rowson-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/06/opinion/04stone-rowson/04stone-rowson-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'ima