ages/2019/11/05/opinion/05callout_web/05callout_web-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 601, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/11/05/opinion/05callout_web/05callout_web-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 601}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/05/opinion/05callout_web/05callout_web-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/05/opinion/05callout_web/05callout_web-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1155, 'width': 1154, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/05/opinion/05callout_web/05callout_web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/11/05/opinion/05callout_web/05callout_web-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/05/opinion/05callout_web/05callout_web-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Invitation to Readers: Variations on a Letter', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Variations on Letters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Letters', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-11-05T17:41:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Letters', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/be42f54f-6c72-52c9-9aee-5159a75c77fd', 'word_count': 92, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be42f54f-6c72-52c9-9aee-5159a75c77fd'}, {'abstract': 'A Shanghai soup dumpling restaurant in a Queens complex, a seasonal Korean menu from a former Neta chef, and more restaurant news.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/11/05/dining/nyc-restaurant-news.html', 'snippet': 'A Shanghai soup dumpling restaurant in a Queens complex, a seasonal Korean menu from a former Neta chef, and more restaurant news.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This addition to the Hudson Yards complex is about as sleek and tailored as a tavern can be. Designed by David Rockwell, it has polished wood, butterscotch leather and a soaring bar with multiple wine racks. It also provides a view of the Vessel, the complex’s centerpiece. The restaurant’s creators, Kenneth A. Himmel, a developer and restaurateur, with Marvin R. Shanken, the publisher of Wine Spectator and other magazines, describe it as an American brasserie. The chef, Eli Kaimeh, of Gramercy Tavern and Per Se, has responded in turn, creating a menu featuring a chopped salad, beef tartare, fancy pork and beans, steak-frites, herb-crusted sea bass, rotisserie chicken and a chocolate-orange creamsicle dessert. “We hope to appeal to the broadest spectrum possible,” Mr. Himmel said. “I believe that you shouldn’t be challenged by a menu.” Mr. Shanken has worked with the beverage director, the master sommelier Michaël Engelmann, to create the elaborate wine list. It includes a nice selection of wines by the glass and the half-bottle, as well as a large assortment of top-flight spirits. A second floor member-only dining club is to open in a few weeks. (Opens Nov. 13) ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/06/dining/05off/merlin_163742484_7babcdb9-6f95-4648-820a-41116d14361e-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600