tury South.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LaPLACE, La. — The New York artist Dread Scott was standing in a tiny traffic island in this working-class suburb west of New Orleans on a recent afternoon near the EZ Stop convenience store. He had come to point out a single sentence on a historical marker, one unheeded by the truck drivers barreling down Airline Highway: “Major 1811 slave uprising organized here.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/07/arts/06slaverevolt2/06slaverevolt2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 841, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/11/07/arts/06slaverevolt2/06slaverevolt2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 841}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/07/arts/06slaverevolt2/06slaverevolt2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 731, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/07/arts/06slaverevolt2/06slaverevolt2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1462, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/07/arts/06slaverevolt2/06slaverevolt2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/11/07/arts/06slaverevolt2/06slaverevolt2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/07/arts/06slaverevolt2/06slaverevolt2-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'With a Slave Rebellion Re-enactment, an Artist Revives Forgotten History', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Marching On, 208 Years Later', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery (Historical)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Historical Re-enactments', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Monuments and Memorials (Structures)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Louisiana', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scott, Dread (Artist)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-11-06T19:46:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Fausset and Wayne Lawrence', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fausset', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Wayne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lawrence', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/75b4a820-44cf-51ac-9d1e-b937a90a3648', 'word_count': 1900, 'uri': 'nyt://article/75b4a820-44cf-51ac-9d1e-b937a90a3648'}, {'abstract': 'Two sisters enter combat in Tochi Onyebuchi’s “War Girls,” a futuristic replay of the Nigerian conflict. Cue the flying robot warriors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/11/06/books/review/war-girls-tochi-onyebuchi.html', 'snippet': 'Two sisters enter combat in Tochi Onyebuchi’s “War Girls,” a futuristic replay of the Nigerian conflict. Cue the flying robot warriors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Onyii is all of 15, but she is battle-worn on every level. She sports a robotic arm — her human one was severed in conflict — and worries she’ll lose her sister, Ify, at any minute. As one of the oldest in a camp of rebel girls based in southeastern Nigeria in the year 2172, she’s an authority on wartime survival. Nigeria is in the grips of civil war, one very similar to the real Biafran War that ravaged the Igbo tribe in 1967-70 as they attempted to secede from Nigeria. In WAR GIRLS (Razorbill, 464 pp., $17.99; ages 12 and up) a series opener from Tochi Onyebuchi (“Beasts Made of Night”), the conflict drags on. Factor in climate change, nuclear disasters and space colonies, and the ultimate dystopian universe reveals itself — part tech-slick playground, part cautionary tale.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '33', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/10/books/review/10-BKS-WAPPLER/10-BKS-WAPPLER-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/11/10/books/review/10-BKS-WAPPLER/10-BKS-WAPPLER-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/10/books/review/10-BKS-WAPPLER/10-BKS-WAPPLER-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/10/books/review/10-BKS-WAPPLER/10-BKS-WAPPLER-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/10/books/review/10-BKS-WAPPLER/10-BKS-WAPPLER-thumbSta