5da8-93e0-082b40cf9a73', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/508f502a-3d00-5da8-93e0-082b40cf9a73'}, {'abstract': 'As they prepare to open their own ambitious farm-to-table project, James Henry and Shaun Kelly have discovered a sideline as sought-after producers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/11/07/t-magazine/james-henry-shaun-kelly-le-doyenne.html', 'snippet': 'As they prepare to open their own ambitious farm-to-table project, James Henry and Shaun Kelly have discovered a sideline as sought-after producers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At around 8 a.m. on a recent Tuesday morning, the Australian chefs James Henry, 36, and Shaun Kelly, 35, fired up a mud-splattered Renault van and set off from the French village of Saint-Vrain, where they live, to Paris, 30 miles north. Once a week, for the past six months, the pair has crawled through the commuter traffic to deliver the produce they harvest from their three-acre vegetable garden to some of the capital’s most exciting restaurants. Today, crates of heirloom carrots, Hokkaido pumpkins, icicle radishes, white beets, Korean Nogak cucumbers and sprays of coriander are destined for the kitchens at chef Bertrand Grébaut’s Michelin-starred bistro Septime, the wildly popular market-driven cafe Mokonuts and the chef Iñaki Aizpitarte’s trailblazing neo-bistro Le Chateaubriand, among others.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/06/t-magazine/food/6tmag-Jameshenry-slide-ZSV8/6tmag-Jameshenry-slide-ZSV8-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/11/06/t-magazine/food/6tmag-Jameshenry-slide-ZSV8/6tmag-Jameshenry-slide-ZSV8-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'imag