the sea that surrounds it; tourists tend to gaze outward, at the waves, toward what separates them from the world they’ve left behind. But on Kauai, at the northwestern end of the chain, it’s the mountains that command the eye, streaked with waterfalls and so furrowed that in satellite photos they look like fossils. Here, on the north shore, the flanks of tall Mamalahoa are steaming, night rains turning to mist in the morning sun. Thousands of feet down lies the river plain of Waipa and its flooded fields filled with leaves like broad, crinkled hearts, each larger than a human face. Among the thick, blushing stalks, a snail.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '122', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/07/t-magazine/07tmag-hawaii-slide-FTE9/07tmag-hawaii-slide-FTE9-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 210, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/11/07/t-magazine/07tmag-hawaii-slide-FTE9/07tmag-hawaii-slide-FTE9-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 210}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/07/t-magazine/07tmag-hawaii-slide-FTE9/07tmag-hawaii-slide-FTE9-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 359, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/07/t-magazine/07tmag-hawaii-slide-FTE9/07tmag-hawaii-slide-FTE9-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 717, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/07/t-magazine/07tmag-hawaii-slide-FTE9/07tmag-hawaii-slide-FTE9-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'t