rotester, while a man arguing with protesters was set on fire. Both were fighting for their lives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — Hong Kong was a city on edge on Monday following escalating violence, in which one demonstrator was shot point blank by a police officer and one man was doused with a flammable liquid and set on fire after he scolded protesters.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/11/world/11hongkong-new-sub/merlin_164212836_3db257c4-a98a-4a2e-8a41-01b4bf31e976-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/11/11/world/11hongkong-new-sub/merlin_164212836_3db257c4-a98a-4a2e-8a41-01b4bf31e976-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/11/world/11hongkong-new-sub/merlin_164212836_3db257c4-a98a-4a2e-8a41-01b4bf31e976-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/11/world/11hongkong-new-sub/merlin_164212836_3db257c4-a98a-4a2e-8a41-01b4bf31e976-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/11/world/11hongkong-new-sub/11hongkong-new-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/11/11/world/11hongkong-new-sub/11hongkong-new-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/11/world/11hongkong-new-sub/11hongkong-new-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ugly From the Outset: Hong Kong’s Day of Widespread Violence', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Day of Anger And Violence In Hong Kong', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hong Kong Protests (2019)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality, Misconduct and Shootings', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARSON', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hong Kong', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lam, Carrie (1957- )', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-11-11T11:51:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Ives, Elaine Yu and Edward Wong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ives', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/96b2c5fb-89ab-546d-95e3-9562b16ae93e', 'word_count': 1108, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96b2c5fb-89ab-546d-95e3-9562b16ae93e'}, {'abstract': 'We interview two creative people in different fields in one wide-ranging conversation. This time: the producer and the musician.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/11/11/t-magazine/david-byrne-binder.html', 'snippet': 'We interview two creative people in different fields in one wide-ranging conversation. This time: the producer and the musician.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One measure of artistic success is the ability to move the mainstream — to bring once-unimaginable ideas to the widest possible audience. By that metric, few people have been as successful as the musician David Byrne and the producer David Binder. Byrne, of course, headlined the ’80s rock band Talking Heads, which dissolved the barriers between disco and rock, conceptual art and dance pop. Even before going solo in 1989, Byrne’s side projects tentacled into disparate worlds: He collaborated with similarly polymathic artists such as Brian Eno, Robert Wilson and Twyla Tharp; he starred in the writer-director Jonathan Demme’s 1984 documentary “Stop Making Sense”; and two years later, he directed the Robert Altman-on-acid jukebox art film “True Stories.” In the ’90s and beyond, Byrne used his fame to advance and collaborate with a wide range of artists — the world-music acts on his label, Luaka Bop; the D.J. Fatboy Slim on “Here Lies Love,” their 2013 musical about Imelda Marcos; the singer St. Vincent on their joint 2012 album “Love This Giant.” His latest act of radical pop is “American Utopia,” an album and touring rock show (which debuted in 2018) that wears its theme lightly but seriously reimagines space: Mobile musicians hop across a bare stage, dancing to their own music. The work opened on Broadway last month and runs through Feb. 16.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '48', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/17/t-magazine/17tmag-byrne/17tmag-byrne-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 403, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/11/17/t-magazine/17tmag-byrne/17tmag-byrne-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 403}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/17/t-magazine/17tmag-byrne/17tmag-byrne-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 687, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/17/t-magazine/17tmag-byrne/17tmag-byrne-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1374, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/17/t-magazine/17tmag-byrne/17tmag-byrne-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/11/17/t-magazine/17tmag-byrne/17tmag-byrne-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/17/t-magazine/17tmag-byrne/17tmag-byrne-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'David Byrne and David Binder on Breaking Into the Mainstream', 'kicker': 'Admiration Society', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'David Binder &amp; David Byrne', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pop and Rock Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Next Wave Festival', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Academy of Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Binder, David', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Byrne, David', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'American Utopia (Album)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-11-11T11:55:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Boris Kachka', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Boris', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kachka', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a539185a-ee5a-5efc-9ca2-c403a3fd2938', 'word_count': 2508, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a539185a-ee5a-5efc-9ca2-c403a3fd2938'}, {'abstract': 'Democrats seized the governorship from Republicans in Wisconsin, once a laboratory for conservative policymaking, but have so far struggled to get things done.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/11/11/us/wisconsin-divided-government.html', 'snippet': 'Democrats seized the governorship from Republicans in Wisconsin, once a laboratory for conservative policymaking, but have so far struggled to get things done.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MADISON, Wis. — The governor of Wisconsin called a special session last week to debate gun legislation. It resulted in exactly zero new laws, and it lasted less than a minute.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/08/us/00WISCONSIN-vos2/merlin_164036982_c5dca5af-f176-48df-ade1-a84f4246b41d-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/11/08/us/00WISCONSIN-vos2/merlin_164036982_c5dca5af-f176-48df-ade1-a84f4246b41d-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'typ