ame': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ukraine', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-11-12T16:21:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew E. Kramer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Kramer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01a59d46-72e9-583a-8529-78cf883ed2ac', 'word_count': 638, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01a59d46-72e9-583a-8529-78cf883ed2ac'}, {'abstract': 'The beauty and calm of the Aland archipelago is deceptive. Isolation encourages contemplation — but can it offer respite as well?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/11/12/t-magazine/mother-loses-son-travel-coping.html', 'snippet': 'The beauty and calm of the Aland archipelago is deceptive. Isolation encourages contemplation — but can it offer respite as well?', 'lead_paragraph': '“THE ALAND ISLANDS — where?” It was the first question my friends asked when I told them of my plan to travel there. “An archipelago in the Baltic Sea,” I would say, “between Sweden and Finland.” But that didn’t quite seem to explain the place. “There are more than 6,700 islands there, and around 65 of them are inhabitable.” Then their interest would be piqued: “How did you find it?”', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '114', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/11/t-magazine/11tmag-aland-slide-X7RQ-copy/11tmag-aland-slide-X7RQ-copy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/11/11/t-magazine/11tmag-aland-slide-X7RQ-copy/11tmag-aland-slide-X7RQ-copy-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/11/t-magazine/11tmag-aland-slide-X7RQ-copy/11tmag-aland-slide-X7RQ-copy-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/11/t-magazine/11tmag-aland-slide-X7RQ-copy/11tmag-aland-slide-X7RQ-copy-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/11/t-magazine/11tmag-aland-slide-X7RQ-copy/11tmag-aland-slide-X7RQ-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/11/11/t-magazine/11tmag-aland-slide-X7RQ-copy/11tmag-aland-slide-X7RQ-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/11/t-magazine/11tmag-aland-slide-X7RQ-copy/11tmag-aland-slide-X7RQ-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Mother Journeys Through Grief Across Finland’s Many Islands', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Hold Oneself Together', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Grief (Emotion)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Finland', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Scandinavia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Baltic Sea', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Ice (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Aland Island', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-11-12T16:21:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Yiyun Li', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Yiyun', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Li', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b96fd088-d479-567f-bd4c-843722ab3322', 'word_count': 3666, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b96fd088-d479-567f-bd4c-843722ab3322'}, {'abstract': 'The autonomous region of Finland offers secluded lodgings, fresh seafood and miles of serene coastline to explore.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/11/12/t-magazine/aland-islands-travel-guide.html', 'snippet': 'The autonomous region of Finland offers secluded lodgings, fresh seafood and miles of serene coastline to explore.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Situated 25 miles east of the Swedish coast, and officially an autonomous region of southwestern Finland, the Aland archipelago consists of about 6,700 named islands and 20,000 smaller rocky outcrops and islets waiting to be discovered. Conquered by Russia in the early 19th century, Aland was declared a Finnish territory in 1921, but the core of its traditions are Swedish, as is the official language. With the Baltic Sea as its backdrop, the archipelago ranges topographically from barren coastal cliffs to stunning white-sand beaches to forests and fertile farmland. Only 35 of the Aland Islands are inhabited and easily accessible from mainland Sweden and Finland; the central islands are linked to one another by bridges and cable ferries, while car ferries connect the archipelago’s more remote reaches.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/12/t-magazine/11tmag-finlandguide-slide-5029/11tmag-finlandguide-slide-5029-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600