name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Community Service Society of New York', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-11-13T15:40:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'Neediest Cases', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisha Brown', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brown', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/17a57819-4c13-58a1-9b0c-eb64059f1b59', 'word_count': 904, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17a57819-4c13-58a1-9b0c-eb64059f1b59'}, {'abstract': 'The fashion designers Jack McCollough and Lazaro Hernandez have dressed the downtown cool girl for over a decade.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/11/13/t-magazine/proenza-schouler-inspiration.html', 'snippet': 'The fashion designers Jack McCollough and Lazaro Hernandez have dressed the downtown cool girl for over a decade.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jack McCollough and Lazaro Hernandez, designers of the New York label Proenza Schouler, didn’t start out thinking they’d work in fashion. “For a while, I studied glass blowing,” says the Tokyo-born, New Jersey-bred McCollough of his time at the San Francisco Art Institute. Hernandez, who was raised in Miami and digested fashion only through the stacks of magazines at his mother’s beauty salon, was originally a pre-med student at the University of Miami. But both members of the now famous duo ended up transferring to Manhattan’s Parsons School of Design in 1998 and, within a few days of arriving in the city, met at the SoHo nightclub Life. They became a couple three years later, and, in 2002, after completing internships at Michael Kors (Hernandez) and Marc Jacobs (McCollough), debuted a joint thesis collection of slouchy separates and embroidered evening wear that was immediately purchased by Barneys New York. In the years since, the 41-year-olds have gone from stars on the rise to stalwarts, creating consistently wearable clothes that nonetheless experiment with proportion, cut, fabric and form — and that have defined (and dressed) the downtown cool girl for over a decade.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/14/t-magazine/14tmag-proenza-slide-9MYI/14tmag-proenza-slide-9MYI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 481, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/11/14/t-magazine/14tmag-proenza-slide-9MYI/14tmag-proenza-slide-9MYI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 481}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/14/t-magazine/14tmag-proenza-slide-9MYI/14tmag-proenza-slide-9MYI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 821, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/14/t-magazine/14tmag-proenza-slide-9MYI/14tmag-proenza-slide-9MYI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1550, 'width': 1934, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/14/t-magazine/14tmag-proenza-slide-9MYI/14tmag-proenza-slide-9MYI-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/11/14/t-magazine/14tmag-proenza-slide-9MYI/14tmag-proenza-slide-9MYI-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/14/t-magazine/14tmag-proenza-slide-9MYI/14tmag-proenza-slide-9MYI-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main':