xpress', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily S. Rueb', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Rueb', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d9fe6914-1654-5e5b-9864-8f4ffab5a283', 'word_count': 876, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d9fe6914-1654-5e5b-9864-8f4ffab5a283'}, {'abstract': 'What exactly did the United States get for the Turkish leader’s White House visit?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/11/13/opinion/editorials/trump-erdogan-turkey.html', 'snippet': 'What exactly did the United States get for the Turkish leader’s White House visit?', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is every reason President Trump should not have hosted President Recep Tayyip Erdogan of Turkey at the White House, including Turkey’s attack on America’s Kurdish allies in Syria, its purchase of antimissile systems from Russia and the brutal continuing crackdown on Turkish journalists and opposition figures. That was obvious to legislators from both parties who wrote Mr. Trump, urging him to disinvite Mr. Erdogan, and it was no doubt obvious to most members of Mr. Trump’s administration, who are now scrambling to justify the visit as bridge-building to a critical ally.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/15/opinion/13trump-erdogan/merlin_164328879_e71de90c-ef9e-41bb-8308-2f13271d02dc-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/11/15/opinion/13trump-erdogan/merlin_164328879_e71de90c-ef9e-41bb-8308-2f13271d02dc-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/15/opinion/13trum