’s a funny moment in Kevin Wilson’s new novel, “Nothing to See Here” — it’s one of two novels we recommend this week — in which a 10-year-old girl tells her new caretaker that she loves reading but had nothing good to choose from at her grandparents’ house, where she and her twin brother had been living. “What did they have?” her caretaker asks, and the girl answers: “Books about World War II. Two different books about Hitler. Wait, four books about Hitler. And other books about Nazis. And books about Stalin. Patton. People like that.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/17/books/17edchoice1/17edchoice1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 293, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/11/17/books/17edchoice1/17edchoice1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 293}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/17/books/17edchoice1/17edchoice1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 500, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/17/books/17edchoice1/17edchoice1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 662, 'width': 1355, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/17/books/17edchoice1/17edchoice1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/11/17/books/17edchoice1/17edchoice1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/17/books/17edchoice1/17edchoice1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '9 New Books We Recommend This Week', 'kicker': 'Editors’ Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Staff Picks From the Book Review', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-11-14T18:45:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/84de73d5-a0f8-54d9-9709-4347a3d5427a', 'word_count': 1244, 'uri': 'nyt://article/84de73d5-a0f8-54d9-9709-4347a3d5427a'}, {'abstract': 'The documentary looks and listens to the emo-rapper, who died two years ago following an accidental overdose.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/11/14/movies/everybodys-everything-review-lil-peep.html', 'snippet': 'The documentary looks and listens to the emo-rapper, who died two years ago following an accidental overdose.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tales of musicians crushed by fame and drugs have, regrettably, become common to the point of cliché. Yet something feels especially sad about the death of Lil Peep after an accidental overdose in 2017 at 21. The documentary “Everybody’s Everything” is steeped in that sadness, even as it celebrates the rapper’s life. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/15/arts/14everybodys/14everybodys-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/11/15/arts/14everybodys/14everybodys-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'j