David Zwirner gallery in Chelsea, Sherry Turner DeCarava, the show’s curator, stood in front of a buzzing crowd discussing a handful of her late husband’s photographs. It was the most comprehensive display of his images since his 1996 retrospective at the Museum of Modern Art, and a palpable excitement hovered in the space. She spoke with a quiet intensity, moving seamlessly from well-known photographs such as 1949’s “Graduation” (a moment that she said “seized his imagination”) to deeper cuts like “Grass” (1991), an aesthetic victory that demonstrated his interest in “lush tonalities,” and “Silhouette” (1964), a study of the body and of how “anger and ego can contort the physical form.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/08/books/review/08gyarkye1/merlin_164321724_17867607-824b-419f-9986-10dede6c1cb4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 817, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/08/books/review/08gyarkye1/merlin_164321724_17867607-824b-419f-9986-10dede6c1cb4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 817}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/08/books/review/08gyarkye1/merlin_164321724_17867607-824b-419f-9986-10dede6c1cb4-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 752, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/08/books/review/08gyarkye1/merlin_164321724_17867607-824b-419f-9986-10dede6c1cb4-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1968, 'width': 1445, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/08/books/review/08gyarkye1/08gyarkye1-thumbSta