'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Elisabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Malkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7790dd37-8d05-5dc5-ac48-2f63b028805c', 'word_count': 794, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7790dd37-8d05-5dc5-ac48-2f63b028805c'}, {'abstract': 'The 14th season of “It’s Always Sunny in Philadelphia” wraps up on FXX. And a live-action “Lady and the Tramp” is on Disney Plus.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/11/20/arts/television/whats-on-tv-wednesday-its-always-sunny-and-lady-and-the-tramp.html', 'snippet': 'The 14th season of “It’s Always Sunny in Philadelphia” wraps up on FXX. And a live-action “Lady and the Tramp” is on Disney Plus.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IT’S ALWAYS SUNNY IN PHILADELPHIA 10 p.m. on FXX. The Gang closes out Season 14 with two back-to-back episodes, making “It’s Always Sunny in Philadelphia” one of the longest-running live-action comedy series to date. The season, which began with attempts by Mac (Rob McElhenney) and Dennis (Glenn Howerton) to attract women by listing Dennis’s bedroom on Airbnb, is expected to maintain its proudly unrefined comedy in its finale. In Episode 9 — Part 1 of the finale — Dennis and Frank (Danny DeVito) protest the trendy haircuts women are getting at a new salon next door, and in Episode 10 — which was inspired by Samuel Beckett’s “Waiting for Godot” — Dennis and Charlie (Charlie Day) reflect upon their love for laser tag. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/20/arts/20tvcol-alwayssunny/20tvcol-alwayssunny-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/11/20/arts/20tvcol-alwayssunny/20tvcol-a