ing, residents unanimously agreed that apartments are too hot, but they rejected a proposal to set the thermostat to 55 degrees because they didn’t want to violate city law. Does the law prohibit shareholders from voting to set the heat at a temperature that is mutually agreeable, if it is below the legal limit?', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/24/realestate/23Ask/23Ask-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 118, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/11/24/realestate/23Ask/23Ask-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 118}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/24/realestate/23Ask/23Ask-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 201, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/24/realestate/23Ask/23Ask-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/24/realestate/23Ask/23Ask-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/11/24/realestate/23Ask/23Ask-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/24/realestate/23Ask/23Ask-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Must We Follow City Heating Rules if the Apartments Are Too Hot?', 'kicker': 'Ask Real Estate', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Must We Follow City Heating Rules If the Apartments Remain Too Hot?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Heating', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Thermostats', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-11-23T15:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ronda Kaysen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ronda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaysen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2cb39e96-298c-54ae-8e74-d07cd8118c97', 'word_count': 405, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2cb39e96-298c-54ae-8e74-d07cd8118c97'}, {'abstract': 'Pierre Le Guennec said that the artist had given him the artworks and that he had forgotten about them for decades.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/11/23/world/europe/picasso-electrician-convicted.html', 'snippet': 'Pierre Le Guennec said that the artist had given him the artworks and that he had forgotten about them for decades.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — One September day in 2010, a man who said he was the former electrician of Pablo Picasso entered the Paris office of the Picasso estate, wheeling a suitcase filled with 271 of the Spaniard’s artworks.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/24/world/24picasso1/merlin_161413947_4b96fe02-1c06-4183-b945-7c881758b297-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/11/24/world/24picasso1/merlin_161413947_4b96fe02-1c06-4183-b945-7c881758b297-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth'