t Sehgal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Parul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sehgal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/84b2f225-9768-5cde-b7a5-72b6c48f0dcb', 'word_count': 712, 'uri': 'nyt://article/84b2f225-9768-5cde-b7a5-72b6c48f0dcb'}, {'abstract': 'For our art critic, the greatness of black visual culture, past and present, mainstream and outsider, was repeatedly asserted during the 2010s.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/11/24/arts/design/black-artists.html', 'snippet': 'For our art critic, the greatness of black visual culture, past and present, mainstream and outsider, was repeatedly asserted during the 2010s.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What made the 2010s the most thrilling of all the decades I’ve spent in the New York art world was the rising presence of black artists of every ilk, on every front: in museums, commercial galleries, art magazines, private collections and public commissions. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/21/arts/00decade-walker1/merlin_164617395_2deb90c3-e1c6-45ff-b5ae-c1c4735317cb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/11/21/arts/00decade-walker1/merlin_164617395_2deb90c3-e1c6-45ff-b5ae-c1c4735317cb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/21/arts/00decade-walker1/merlin_164617395_2deb90c3-e1c6-45ff-b5ae-c1c4735317cb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/21/arts/00decade-walker1/merlin_164617395_2deb90c3-e1