cle', 'news_desk': 'Well', 'section_name': 'Well', 'subsection_name': 'Family', 'byline': {'original': 'By Debra Weiner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Debra', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weiner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/403256f1-9af1-5d88-b92f-b10beacc3ffb', 'word_count': 1884, 'uri': 'nyt://article/403256f1-9af1-5d88-b92f-b10beacc3ffb'}, {'abstract': 'The album was an emblem of young women’s anger in the 1990s. And now it’s coming to Broadway.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/11/26/magazine/alanis-morissette-jagged-little-pill-musical.html', 'snippet': 'The album was an emblem of young women’s anger in the 1990s. And now it’s coming to Broadway.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Alanis Morissette song “Hand in My Pocket” is a list of opposites that aren’t really opposites at all, when you really think about it. “I’m short but I’m healthy,” she sings, in the half-yodel, half-dove-coo that people still channel in late-night karaoke bars. “I’m sick but I’m pretty, babyyyy.” Being petite has no bearing on a person’s hardiness, but of course, that isn’t the point. Morissette wrote the song — which became the fourth track on “Jagged Little Pill,” an album so popular at its height that it sold nearly half a million copies in one week — in less than an hour when she was 20. The lyrics are more affective than logical, more moody than meticulous. Morissette was showing that young people contain multitudes and that they are eager to describe — to themselves, to some distant, judgmental grown-up — their contradictory inner lives.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/01/magazine/01mag-morissette-image-02/01mag-morissette-image-02-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 905, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/01/magazine/01mag-morissette-image-02/01mag-morissette-image-02-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 905}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/01/magazine/01mag-morissette-image-02/01mag-morissette-image-02-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 679, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/01/magazine/01mag-morissette-image-02/01mag-morissette-image-02-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1358, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/01/magazine/01mag-morissette-image-02/01mag-morissette-image-02-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/12/01/magazine/01mag-morissette-image-02/01mag-morissette-image-02-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/01/magazine/01mag-morissette-image-02/01mag-morissette-image-02-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Alanis Morissette Isn’t Angry Anymore. But ‘Jagged Little Pill’ Rages On.', 'kicker': 'Feature', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Truth Hurts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morisette, Alanis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Jagged Little Pill (Album)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Jagged Little Pill (Play)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pop and Rock Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-11-26T10:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Syme', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Syme', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/67c6dc05-efc2-55a0-9af6-e66c89f5d904', 'word_count': 4254, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67c6dc05-efc2-55a0-9af6-e66c89f5d904'}, {'abstract': 'In a workshop in northwestern France, what starts as a simple wooden box ends up as one of Louis Vuitton’s iconic pieces.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/11/26/t-magazine/louis-vuitton-petite-malle.html', 'snippet': 'In a workshop in northwestern France, what starts as a simple wooden box ends up as one of Louis Vuitton’s iconic pieces.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Louis Vuitton, the man who in 1854 founded the French leather-goods company that bears his name, began his career not as a malletier (trunk maker), as one might imagine, but as one of the world’s most rarefied packers. His first job in Paris involved folding the clothes of Empress Eugénie, the wife of Napoleon III, to fit snugly into her custom travel cases. Once out on his own, Louis Vuitton created a flat-topped, stackable, voyage-ready chest; before that, trunks had rounded lids to protect against the rain.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '98', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/26/t-magazine/26tmag-vuitton/26tmag-vuitton-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 750, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/11/26/t-magazine/26tmag-vuitton/26tmag-vuitton-articleLarge.jpg', 'x