per', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c354816-0cbf-528a-a4a1-86e0d06d4e8a', 'word_count': 1965, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c354816-0cbf-528a-a4a1-86e0d06d4e8a'}, {'abstract': 'Once a father took his aversion to “Frozen” and, yes, let it go, he realized that beyond the branded backpacks and Anna and Elsa matching sock sets, the movie is an ingeniously crafted tale.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/11/27/movies/frozen-2-repeat.html', 'snippet': 'Once a father took his aversion to “Frozen” and, yes, let it go, he realized that beyond the branded backpacks and Anna and Elsa matching sock sets, the movie is an ingeniously crafted tale.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Frozen” was released on Nov. 27, 2013, and our daughter was born nearly two months later, so, even before my wife and I had a child, “Frozen” hovered on the fringes of our parental consciousness as something to be avoided and vaguely despised. If the term “Disney princess” evokes, for a certain kind of parent, a distasteful ethos of sparkly dresses, impossible wasp-waists and fables of princely rescue, then “Frozen,” which centers on not one but two princesses, seemed, sight unseen, to be doubly odious. Yet one day, years later, when preschool was canceled and the rain was unceasing, my wife sent me a text: She’d done it. In case of emergency, she’d broken glass. She’d rented, and they’d watched, “Frozen.”', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/01/arts/01FROZEN-REPEAT1/01FROZEN-REPEAT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 852, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/01/arts/01FROZEN-REPEAT1/01FROZEN-REPEAT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 852}}, {'rank': 