ok about the extramarital kind as well. First the global stuff: Charles Moore concludes his magisterial biography of Margaret Thatcher with a third volume, “Herself Alone”; Thant Myint-U shows how Burma’s colonial history plays into that country’s current spasms toward democracy; James Verini offers a look at the battle against the Islamic State and the long history of the city of Mosul; and the artist Odyr interprets George Orwell’s classic “Animal Farm,” an allegory of authoritarian rule and Stalinist repression. On the home front, Adrienne Brodeur’s memoir, “Wild Game,” recounts how her mother enlisted her as an adolescent to help cover up her torrid affair. Other books we recommend this week include novels by Lara Vapnyar and Carol Anshaw, and memoirs by the nurse Molly Case and the “Queer Eye” grooming expert Jonathan Van Ness.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/01/books/01edchoice/01edchoice-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 301, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/01/books/01edchoice/01edchoice-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 301}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/01/books/01edchoice/01edchoice-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 513, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/01/books/01edchoice/01edchoice-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 657, 'width': 1311, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/01/books/01edchoice/01edchoice-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail