place-them.html', 'snippet': 'Quotation of the Day for Thursday, November 28, 2019.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Many farming families are in the Hulls’ situation or getting near it. They’re land-rich and cash-poor, and they’re saying, ‘What do we do with this family business we’ve created?’ ”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Quotation of the Day: As Farmers Grow Old, Who Will Replace Them?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quote of the Day', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2019-11-28T03:11:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Today’s Paper', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Quote', '_id': 'nyt://article/4945169b-f47f-57ee-aa02-37f96b8754d6', 'word_count': 60, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4945169b-f47f-57ee-aa02-37f96b8754d6'}, {'abstract': 'The video app said it would review its policies after a 17-year-old in New Jersey who discussed Chinese detention camps was locked out of her account.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/11/27/technology/tiktok-censorship-apology.html', 'snippet': 'The video app said it would review its policies after a 17-year-old in New Jersey who discussed Chinese detention camps was locked out of her account.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SHANGHAI — The video app TikTok on Wednesday reversed its decision to block an American teenager who posted a clip in which she discussed the mass internment of minority Muslims in China, and acknowledged that its moderation system had overreached in shutting her out of her account. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/28/world/28tiktok/merlin_163685844_f489b728-babe-44be-9692-38ea214bcbca-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name':