aduate, who is more than capable of standing shoulder-to-shoulder with the show’s previous leading men.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/28/arts/28londontheater01/merlin_165019347_7bd073b7-179a-41b1-9e80-775d416b718e-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/11/28/arts/28londontheater01/merlin_165019347_7bd073b7-179a-41b1-9e80-775d416b718e-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/28/arts/28londontheater01/merlin_165019347_7bd073b7-179a-41b1-9e80-775d416b718e-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/28/arts/28londontheater01/merlin_165019347_7bd073b7-179a-41b1-9e80-775d416b718e-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/28/arts/28londontheater01/28londontheater01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/11/28/arts/28londontheater01/28londontheater01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/28/arts/28londontheater01/28londontheater01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Musicals of Various Shapes and Sizes Arrive on London Stages', 'kicker': 'THEATER REVIEW', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Dear Evan Hansen (Play)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Ghost Quartet (Play)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-11-28T10:19:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Wolf', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wolf', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e921ef17-18cf-5703-a7ec-ca04d15612f0', 'word_count': 1038, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e921ef17-18cf-5703-a7ec-ca04d15612f0'}, {'abstract': 'He says he doesn’t want to think about the future. That’s all I can think about.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/11/28/style/should-i-break-up-with-my-boyfriend.html', 'snippet': 'He says he doesn’t want to think about the future. That’s all I can think about.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My boyfriend of four years is everything I want in a man — intelligent, kind and adventurous — and I would really like to get married. We’ve discussed it several times. He’s made it clear that he loves me, but is not interested in thinking about the future because the concept of his mortality terrifies him. While I am happy in our daily life, I have made sacrifices to keep us together (two cross-country moves for his career), but he has not shown a similar long-term commitment to me. Should I walk away from a man I love because he can’t commit, or should I stay and enjoy my happiness?', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 599}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 923, 'width': 925, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 923, 'width': 925, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Should I Break Up With My Boyfriend?', 'kicker': 'SOCIAL Q’S', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Secure Future', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customs, Etiquette and Manners', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-11-28T11:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip Galanes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Galanes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3001c732-f151-52a8-a6a1-5be7251065f8', 'word_count': 857, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3001c732-f151-52a8-a6a1-5be7251065f8'}, {'abstract': 'Fred Rogers’s belief that we should validate emotions, not suppress them, is wisdom for all ages.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/11/28/opinion/mister-rogers-neighborhood.html', 'snippet': 'Fred Rogers’s belief that we should validate emotions, not suppress them, is wisdom for all ages.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the 51st anniversary of the first taping of the classic children’s show “Mister Rogers’ Neighborhood,” Google published an animated “doodle” commemorating him. It depicts Mister Rogers walking through the neighborhood interacting with a variety of people, including a small child with his head hung low. Rogers fashions a paper airplane for the boy, which instantly cheers him up. People commonly caricature Mister Rogers this way — a gentle man intent on making everyone happy — but that may be more a reflection of America’s discomfort with dark emotions than of the man himself. The last thing Fred Rogers would do for a sad boy is distract him from his sadness.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/28/opinion/28stone-alessandri/28stone-alessandri-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/11/28/opinion/28stone-alessandri/28stone-alessandri-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/28/opinion/28stone-alessandri/28stone-alessandri-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'cr