e}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ecf94d4-e093-56b9-b5f8-ae32c144e8f6', 'word_count': 432, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ecf94d4-e093-56b9-b5f8-ae32c144e8f6'}, {'abstract': 'Reading books by Latina writers taught me our stories were worthy of being told.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/11/30/opinion/latina-writers.html', 'snippet': 'Reading books by Latina writers taught me our stories were worthy of being told.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I grew up in Bushwick, Brooklyn, in the 1980s, in what felt like a forgotten neighborhood. Abandoned buildings loomed over piles of garbage and rubble. Playgrounds were overrun by drug dealers. But for me, Bushwick was a place imbued with my culture. There were piragua carts with multicolored umbrellas selling shaved ice on every corner. The bodeguero Miguel gave my mother credit when our food stamps ran out. The Puerto Rican flag hung from almost every window. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/02/opinion/30martir/30martir-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 409, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/12/02/opinion/30martir/30martir-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 409}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/02/opinion/30martir/30martir-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 698, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/02/opinion/30martir/30martir-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1396, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/12/02/opinion/30martir/30martir-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'w